\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RprepEMPTYsubst1
< m1: I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                         [ mu7,
                           head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu6],
                           I2:mu2,
                           prepobjrel/I4::EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1},
                           I3:mu3
                         ],
                   mu4
                 ]

  SUBST:
  m2: EMPTY(KEY_zeroEMPTYkey){EMPTYrec1} 
>

< m : I9::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec2}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                         [ mu7,
                           head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu6],
                           I2:mu2,
                           I5:mu3
                         ],
                  mu4
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_emptyPrepobjVps <> []
       I2: ALL (mu2, I21:rel1/..)
         I21: rel1 IN AUX_prePrepobjVPrels AND 
              rel1 <> prepobjrel
       I3: ALL (mu3, I31:rel2/..)
       I4: EMPTYVARrec1.index = LEVEL
       I5: ALL (mu3, I31:rel2/..) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [../EMPTYVAR{}]) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [../VARPREPP{} [ I51:mu5] ])
         I31: rel2 IN AUX_postPrepobjVPrels
         I51: EXIST (mu5, [../EMPTYVAR{}])
       I9: CLAUSErec2.synvpefs * AUX_emptyPrepobjVps <> []
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: EMPTYVARrec1 = { person : 3
                               number : omeganumber
                               gender : omegagender }
              (* same as zeroEMPTY *) 
          A1: CLAUSErec2 := CLAUSErec1;
              CLAUSErec2.synvpefs := 
                        CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_emptyPrepobjVps
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: EMPTYVARrec1 := { index  : LEVEL
                                person : 3
                                number : omeganumber
                                gender : omegagender };
              CLAUSErec1 := CLAUSErec2;
              CLAUSErec1.synvpefs := 
                        CLAUSErec2.synvpefs * AUX_emptyPrepobjVps

         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RprepEMPTYsubst1
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item synDONP\_EMPTY:\\
(vp123): x1 told the whole story EMPTY $\rightarrow$ x1 told the whole story
\item synCLOSEDADJPPROP\_EMPTY:\\
(vp012): -- seem x1 ill EMPTY $\rightarrow$ seem x1 ill
\item synCLOSEDNPPROP\_EMPTY:\\
(vp012): -- seem x1 a fool EMPTY $\rightarrow$ seem x1 a fool\\
In the formulation of the sets prePrepobj and postPrepobjVPrels in the 
auxdomain it is assumed that all complrels occurring before a prepobj have by 
now been converted to predrels, while those following a prepobj have remained 
complrels.
\item synEMPTY\_PREPNP:\\
(vp123): x1 talked EMPTY about a new car $\rightarrow$ x1 talked about a new 
car\\
If the model contains two prepobjrels, this rule covers only the case where the 
first one is EMPTY (note that the sets of prePrepobj and postPrepobjVPrels in 
the auxdomain both contain a prepobjrel, since in theory the difference between 
the two cannot be seen). In case the second prepobjrel contains the EMPTY, a 
separate rule is needed (RprepEMPTYsubst2). Remember that in the pattern rules 
only one canonical argument order is allowed: x1 talk to x3 about x2 , i.e.\ 
first the goal (`indirect object'), then the theme (`direct object').
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) 
RC\_EMPTYsubstitution
\item[* date of creation:] 17-MAR-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] --
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] delete a prepobj when there is an EMPTY as substituent (in 
generation)
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] In analysis, a condition 
has been formulated on the order of EMPTYsubst-rule application: the leftmost 
EMPTYVAR must be introduced first. This prevents many (correct) ambiguities, 
caused only by a different order of application of the substitution rules. Now, 
all orders of application leaving some EMPTYVARs unintroduced will be filtered 
out later (probably only in the pattern rules, where the number of arguments is 
counted) as incorrect.\\
In generation, the order of substitution is determined by the order found in 
the source language.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that the zeroEMPTYkey is the best choice here; Dutch has a 
oneEMPTY in case of the AanActive, (hij liet het boek (aan EMPTY) zien), so 
that the mapping for EMPTYs must be taken care of. Probably, there is only one 
zero/menEMPTY in IL, mapping on both possibilities in the separate languages.
The value used here must match with indobjEMPTY too. Note that the 
attribute values of the EMPTY matter for NOC and for 
reflexives: he ordered (zeroEMPTY) to behave {\em one\/}self/selves; he ordered 
that {\em one\/} go home(?) $\leftarrow$ hij beval (zeroEMPTY) (BIGPRO) naar 
huis te gaan
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
